DATASET:
Name: Credit Card Fraud Detection (KAGGLE)
Link: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets/mlg-ulb/creditcardfraud
Rows and Columns: 284807 rows, 31 columns
Discription: This dataset contains credit card transactions made by european cardholders in September 2013, with a total of 492 fraudulent transactions out of 284,807 records.


UNDERSTANDING THE DATASET:
Each row: A single credit card transaction made.
Most important and useful columns: Time, Amount, and V1 through V28.
Target variable: Class (0 for legit, 1 for fraud)
Class distribution: Most of the transactions belogn to the legit class, with only a tiny percentage belonging to the fraud class.


FEAUTURES AND TARGET:
Features: Time, Amount, and V1 through V28.
Target: Class (0 = legit and 1 = fraud).


TRAINING AND TESTIING DATA SPLIT:
Train/Test Split: The dataset was split into an 80/20 ratio (80% for training data, 20% for testing data).
Why we seperate them: Testing data needs to be kept separate to evaluate how well the model does with new data, which prevents the model from simply memorizing training patterns and helps avoid overfitting.


LOGISTIC REGRESSION MODEL:

import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
from sklearn.model_selection import train_test_split
from sklearn.linear_model import LogisticRegression
from sklearn.metrics import accuracy_score, confusion_matrix, classification_report

#Loading the Dataset
df = pd.read_csv("/content/drive/MyDrive/AI100/FraudDetection.csv")

#Defining Features and Target
features = [
    "Time", "Amount", "V1", "V2", "V3", "V4", "V5", "V6", "V7", "V8",
    "V9", "V10", "V11", "V12", "V13", "V14", "V15", "V16", "V17", "V18",
    "V19", "V20", "V21", "V22", "V23", "V24", "V25", "V26", "V27", "V28"
]

X = df[features]
y = df["Class"]

#Spliting the Data
X_train, X_test, y_train, y_test = train_test_split(X, y, test_size=0.20, random_state=42)

#Creating and Training the Model
model = LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)
model.fit(X_train, y_train)

#Making the prediction
y_pred = model.predict(X_test)


MODEL EVALUATION:
Accuracy: 99.90%
Precision: 0.81 (When the model flagged a transaction as fraud, it was correct 81% of the time)
Recall: 0.52 (The model caught 52% of all actual fraud cases)
F1-Score: 0.63 (A balanced mean of precision and recall)

CONFUSION MATRIX:
True Negatives: 56,852 (Legit transactions correctly identified as legit)
False Positives: 12 (Legit transactions incorrectly flagged as fraud)
False Negatives: 47 (Fraud transactions missed and flagged as legit)
True Positives: 51 (Fraud transactions correctly identified as fraud)
